 ## Running safely
 
+- Launch only a development build (bundle id ending in `.dev`, built with
+  `build-macos.sh` without `BUNDLE_ID`). macOS keeps one screen recording,
+  camera and microphone grant per bundle id: launching a build that shares
+  `local.livehime.macos` with the installed LiveHime takes that grant over,
+  and the installed app takes it back, so each one loses screen capture.
+  Check release builds without launching them (the `scripts/verify-*`
+  checks); test the same sources under the `.dev` id.
+  Every copy of a release build left on disk (unpacked zips, mounted DMGs,
+  build folders) is registered under that id too, and a grant can end up on
+  any of them: afterwards move them to the Trash and unregister them
+  (`lsregister -u <path>`) so only the installed app remains.
+- Build with `build-macos.sh`, not `cmake --build` alone: only the script
+  signs with the local certificate. An ad hoc signature changes with every
+  build, and macOS drops the grants of an ad hoc app it has seen before.
 - Do not run while a normal LiveHime is open: `open --env` reuses the running
   instance instead of starting an isolated one.
 - The unreachable-server case leaves OBS's modal "failed to connect" dialog
